  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":56,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A water tank is being filled at a steady rate. After 4 minutes it holds 260 litres and after 9 minutes it holds 460 litres. Find the rate of change, in litres per minute.\\n\\nb Find how much water the tank held at the start, and how long it takes to hold 1 100 litres. Explain what your answers mean in this context.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A water tank is being filled at a steady rate. After 4 minutes it holds 260 litres and after 9 minutes it holds 460 litres. Find the rate of change, in litres per minute."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Find how much water the tank held at the start, and how long it takes to hold 1 100 litres. Explain what your answers mean in this context."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Rate = (460 − 260) ÷ (9 − 4) = 200 ÷ 5 = 40 litres per minute — the volume grows linearly, 40 L every minute. (b) Volume(t) = start + 40t. Using (4, 260): start = 260 − 160 = 100 litres — the tank held 100 L before filling began (e.g. a base reserve). Time to 1100 L: 100 + 40t = 1100 → t = 25 minutes — 25 minutes after filling starts the tank holds 1100 L.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the method (rise ÷ run with 460 − 260 and 9 − 4), 1 for 40 L/min with units. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the starting 100 litres, 1 for t = 25 minutes, 1 for the contextual explanation (base amount + time measured from the start of filling).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘40 litres/min’, ‘40 L/min’; explanation phrased any way that identifies 100 L as the t = 0 level and 25 min from the start of filling.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":56,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Slope between the two known points first, then rewind to t = 0.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) rate of change","prompt":"Find th